00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:03.830 align:start position:0%
 
hi everyone  this is professor mdos science and today  i want to talk about identical particles  in another one of our videos on rigorous  quantum mechanics identical particles  are easy to understand intuitively  two particles are identical if we give  them the same name for example  all electrons are identical to each  other and all protons are identical to  each other  however electrons are not identical to  protons  at this point you may be wondering sure  but it is rather obvious so what's the  fuss  if you have a system of identical  particles then exchanging  any two particles will lead to exactly  the same physics  this simple statement has profound  implications and they are  vastly different from classical to  quantum physics  in classical physics identical particles  are distinguishable  but in quantum physics identical  particles are  indistinguishable today we will learn  what the precise meaning of these words  is  and that will prepare us to study  systems of many quantum particles  so let's go let's start with the  definition of identical particles  two particles are identical if their  intrinsic defining properties are the  same  so what do i mean by intrinsic  properties  these are the properties that you list  when you want to characterize the type  of particle  and include things like its mass its  charge its spin or its magnetic moment  with this definition all electrons are  identical to each other  because they have the same mass the same  charge the same spin  and so on you can consider another  elementary particle  for example the muon and again all muons  are identical to all other  muons because they have the same  intrinsic defining properties  however muons and electrons are not  identical  because they have the same charge and  the same spin but they don't have  the same mass another example of an  elementary particle is the positron  it is the anti-particle of the electron  and as such it has the same mass  and the same spin but a different charge  so though all positrons are identical  amongst themselves they're not identical  to electrons  we can in fact move beyond elementary  particles to find other families of  identical particles  all protons are identical although they  are not elementary in that they are made  of quarks  similarly all hydrogen atoms are  identical to each other although they  are made of a proton and an electron  and you can continue this way to define  other sets of identical particles  going back to electrons for the sake of  argument note that identical  doesn't mean that electrons are in the  same quantum state  all electrons are identical in that they  all have the same mass the same charge  and so on  but identical particles may be in  different states  for example the electrons may be moving  with different momenta or one may be  bound to a proton to form hydrogen  while another may be free so it is  important to understand this distinction  all electrons are identical but  electrons may be in different states  from a practical point of view what this  means is that exchanging two identical  particles  doesn't affect the properties of the  system  the consequences of this statement are  vastly different for classical and  quantum particles  and this is what i want to explore in  the rest of the video  let's start with identical classical  particles  consider a room such as the one shown  here and i will label the corners as red  blue orange and green  then we place two identical particles  here and here  we give them some velocity so that they  initially move towards each other like  this  then they collide and change direction  and eventually end up here  and here if we look at this situation it  is clear that although these two  particles are identical  in the sense that they have the same  mass charge and so on we can nonetheless  distinguish them  if i call the particle that is starting  near the red corner particle one  and the particle that's starting near  the blue corner particle two  then i can say that in our experiment  particle 1 went from the red corner to  the orange corner  and particle 2 went from the blue corner  to the green corner  so why can i do this i can do this  because i can track the exact  path that particle 1 follows and the  exact path that particle two follows  so at every single point in time i know  where each of the two particles is  so in some sense the labels one and two  that i gave to these particles based on  where they started  behave like intrinsic labels that in  fact allow me to tell the particles  apart  in other words classical identical  particles  are distinguishable if we first exchange  the particles and then run the  experiment  then particle 1 now would be the one  going from the blue corner  to the green corner and particle 2 would  be the one going from the red corner  to the orange corner this is an explicit  demonstration that we can distinguish  these two situations however the physics  of the problem remains the same  when we exchange identical particles if  somebody walked into the room  right before the measurement but didn't  know whether we exchanged the position  of the particles or we didn't  then they wouldn't be able to decide  between these two scenarios because the  physics is the same  so what happens for identical quantum  particles  in this case the situation is  fundamentally different our ability to  distinguish two identical classical  particles is based on the fact that we  can exactly follow the individual  trajectories of these particles at all  times  this is impossible in quantum mechanics  to understand why let's look at a  concrete example  let's imagine two wave packets  describing two identical quantum  particles here  and here these wave packets don't  overlap in a space to  start with so we can initially assign a  label 1 to this particle  and the label 2 to this particle just  like we did for the classical particles  let's again consider a collision between  two particles and in this case consider  the collision in the center of mass  frame  at the initial time t0 we have two well  separated wave packets that don't  overlap  so we can definitely label the particle  on the left as particle 1 and the  particle on the right as particle 2.  just like in classical physics even  though these two particles are identical  their states are different and we can  label them  the particles that move towards each  other and at some later time t1  they will start to overlap during the  collision  the wave packets overlap remembering the  videos on a scattering  at a later time t2 after the collision  we have a combined wave packet of the  two particles that spreads over time  in all directions then imagine that we  place the detector here  and we detect a particle moving outward  in this direction  conservation of momentum immediately  tells us that the other particle is  moving in the opposite direction  but which particle is which when the  wave packets overlap we can no longer  track  particle one or particle two so what  does this mean quantum identical  particles  are indistinguishable  this collision example makes the wave  packet overlap very clear  but any wave packets that don't overlap  to start with will eventually overlap  due to the time evolution of the system  when that happens if we detect a  particle in a region  in which the wave packets overlap we  have no way of telling which of the two  particles it was  what this means is that identical  classical and quantum particles behave  in radically different ways  identical classical particles are  distinguishable  whereas identical quantum particles are  indistinguishable  before we wrap up i want to make two  extra comments first  these diagrams here are very schematic  so you should not take them too  seriously  the state of a two-particle system for  example its wavefunction the position  representation  is described by a six-variable function  which we clearly cannot draw  second i want to emphasize that this is  a fundamental result  not a limitation of how measuring  equipment quantum particles that are  identical  are indistinguishable this has profound  implications for the physics of many  particle systems  and i explored them in many other videos  that you can find in the description  quantum identical particles are  indistinguishable and this has some  profound implications  if you want to see one of the most  mind-blowing ones check out the video on  exchange degeneracy  if you like this video or like send me  suggestions for future videos please  subscribe

